WEBVTT

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:11.000
<v Ira Glass>There's this moment during the Cuban Missile Crisis that's so incredible, it doesn't sound real. But we have confirmed this with people who were there. Victor Gilinsky wrote about this for the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists.
<v Ira Glass>在古巴导弹危机期间，有这样一个时刻，它是如此令人难以置信，听起来都不真实。但我们已经从当时在场的人那里证实了这一点。《原子科学家公报》的维克多·吉林斯基对此进行了撰文。

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:31.520
<v Ira Glass>He said, basically, nuclear war seemed so imminent that the underground headquarters of the Strategic Air Command in Nebraska went into lock down. Nobody was allowed to enter or leave. He said the General in charge told the staff he expected an order to launch nuclear missiles any moment, and also expected that they would all die from a Soviet counterattack.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当时核战争似乎迫在眉睫，内布拉斯加州战略空军司令部的地下指挥部进入了封锁状态。任何人都不得进出。他说，负责的将军告诉工作人员，他随时准备接到发射核导弹的命令，也预料到他们所有人都会死于苏联的反击。

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:42.220
<v Ira Glass>And everybody was allowed to make one phone call home to their family. But in that call, they were not allowed to say why they were calling. What do you say in that situation?
<v Ira Glass>每个人都被允许给家人打一个电话。但在那个电话里，他们不被允许说出为什么要打电话。在这种情况下，你能说什么呢？

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:56.080
<v Ira Glass>Gilinsky writes, "The conversations were about scraped kids' knees and sick dogs. It was a scene straight out of Dr. Strangelove ." And of course, right? What can words do when you're staring death in the face?
<v Ira Glass>吉林斯基写道：“谈话内容都是关于孩子擦伤的膝盖和生病的狗。那场景简直就像电影《奇爱博士》里的情节。”当然，不是吗？当你面对死亡时，言语又能起到什么作用呢？

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:08.160
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, we have two stories where ordinary people rise to this exact task. They see death standing there, and they open their mouths. And truly amazing things happen.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们将讲述两个普通人应对这一任务的故事。他们看见死亡就在那里，于是他们开口了。真正令人惊叹的事情发生了。

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:27.030
<v Ira Glass>We have two stories for you today. And to say anything else about these stories, I think, would be a spoiler. Also, I want to give every possible second to these stories. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And let's just get to it with the first act of our show.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们为您准备了两个故事。我认为，关于这些故事再说任何话都会是一种剧透。另外，我想把每一秒钟都留给这些故事。这里是来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。让我们直接进入节目的第一幕吧。

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:34.470
<v Ira Glass>So remember that tsunami that hit Japan five years ago, March 2011? Giant black waves more than 30-feet tall hit Japan.
<v Ira Glass>还记得五年前，也就是 2011 年 3 月袭击日本的那场海啸吗？高达 30 多英尺的巨大黑色海浪袭击了日本。

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.580
<v Ira Glass>[JAPANESE PEOPLE YELLING]
<v Ira Glass>[日本人的叫喊声]

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.560
<v Ira Glass>A guy in a little town called Otsuchi shot this video. You can hear him yelling.
<v Ira Glass>一个叫大槌町的小镇上的人拍下了这段视频。你可以听到他在大喊。

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.740
<v Ira Glass>[JAPANESE PEOPLE YELLING]
<v Ira Glass>[日本人的叫喊声]

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.390
<v Ira Glass>All the houses are washing away.
<v Ira Glass>所有的房子都被冲走了。

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.790
<v Ira Glass>[JAPANESE PEOPLE YELLING]
<v Ira Glass>[日本人的叫喊声]

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:50.030
<v Ira Glass>Otsuchi had been there for 100 years. In 30 minutes, it was gone-- almost totally flattened.
<v Ira Glass>大槌町已经存在 100 年了。在 30 分钟内，它消失了——几乎被完全夷为平地。

00:01:50.030 --> 00:02:05.830
<v Ira Glass>The tsunami and the earthquake that went with it killed six times more people than died in 9/11-- over 19,000 people. Another 2,500 are still missing. And in the aftermath, of course, families struggled to figure out how they were going to move forward without the people they loved.
<v Ira Glass>那场海啸以及随之而来的地震造成的死亡人数是 9·11 事件的六倍——超过 19,000 人。另有 2,500 人至今下落不明。当然，在余波中，家庭都在努力寻找如何带着对逝者的爱继续前行。

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:18.350
<v Ira Glass>And in that town of Otsuchi, it lead to this new-- I don't know, ritual is not exactly the right word for this. But it's something close to that-- this thing that people invented to stay connected to the dead. One of our producers, Miki Meek, has family in Japan.
<v Ira Glass>在大槌町，这导致了一种新的——我不知道，“仪式”这个词并不完全准确。但这很接近——这是人们发明出来与死者保持联系的东西。我们的制作人之一，美纪·米克，她在日本有家人。

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:31.200
<v Ira Glass>And she grew up going back and forth between there and here. And she watched this documentary about this thing people are doing Otsuchi, on the Japanese news channel, NHK, and got permission for us to play you some excerpts. Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>她从小就在日本和这里之间往返。她在日本新闻频道 NHK 上看到了关于人们在大槌町所做的事情的纪录片，并获得了播放片段的许可。这是美纪。

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:52.530
<v Miki Meek>Of all the areas in Japan affected by that tsunami, Otsuchi has one of the highest numbers of missing people-- 421. Today, it's still partly in ruins and partly a construction site, as they try to rebuild the town on higher ground. But a year before that tsunami happened, this guy named Itaru Sasaki-- he was already dealing with a loss.
<v Miki Meek>在日本所有受海啸影响的地区中，大槌町的失踪人数最多——421 人。今天，那里仍有一部分是废墟，一部分是建筑工地，因为他们试图在高地上重建城镇。但在那场海啸发生的前一年，一个叫佐佐木格的人——他已经在应对失去亲人的痛苦了。

00:02:52.530 --> 00:03:00.710
<v Miki Meek>His cousin had just died. And Itaru was having a hard time figuring out how to talk about it. So he did something pretty ingenious.
<v Miki Meek>他的表亲刚去世。佐佐木格很难找到表达哀悼的方式。所以他做了一件非常有创意的事情。

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:11.120
<v Miki Meek>He went out and bought an old-fashioned phone booth and stuck it in his garden. It looks like an old English-style one. It's square and painted white, and has these glass window panes.
<v Miki Meek>他出去买了一个老式的电话亭，把它放在他的花园里。它看起来像那种旧式的英国电话亭。它是方形的，漆成白色，有玻璃窗格。

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:18.540
<v Miki Meek>Inside is a black rotary phone, resting on a wood shelf. This phone connected to nowhere. It didn't work at all.
<v Miki Meek>里面是一部黑色的转盘电话，放在木架上。这台电话没有连接任何地方。它根本不能使用。

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:34.010
<v Miki Meek>But that didn't matter to Itaru. He just needed a place where he felt like he could talk to his cousin, a place where he could air out his grief. And so putting an old phone booth in his garden, which sits on this little windy hill overlooking the Pacific Ocean, it felt like a perfect solution.
<v Miki Meek>但这对佐佐木格来说并不重要。他只是需要一个地方，让他觉得可以和他的表亲交谈，一个可以宣泄悲伤的地方。所以，在俯瞰太平洋的一个多风小山丘上的花园里放一个旧电话亭，这感觉像是一个完美的解决方案。

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.790
<v Itaru Sasaki>[JAPANESE]
<v Itaru Sasaki>[日语]

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:45.570
<v Miki Meek>He's saying, because my thoughts could not be relayed over a regular phone line, I wanted them to be carried on the wind.
<v Miki Meek>他说，因为我的思念无法通过普通的电话线传递，我想让它们随风而去。

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.890
<v Itaru Sasaki>[JAPANESE]
<v Itaru Sasaki>[日语]

00:03:47.890 --> 00:04:02.100
<v Miki Meek>So I named it the wind telephone-- kaze no denwa. The idea of keeping up a relationship with the dead is not such a strange one in Japan. The line between our world and their world is thin.
<v Miki Meek>所以我把它命名为“风之电话”——Kaze no denwa。在日本，与死者保持联系的想法并不奇怪。我们世界与他们世界之间的界限很薄。

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:20.010
<v Miki Meek>Lots of families keep a Buddhist altar for their dead relatives in the living room. My uncle has one for our family. There are photos on a little platform. And every day, he leaves fresh fruit and rice for them, lights incense, and rings a bell. It's a way to stay in touch, to let them know that they're still a big part of our family.
<v Miki Meek>许多家庭在客厅里为死去的亲人供奉佛龛。我的叔叔为我们的家庭设了一个。小平台上放着照片。每天，他都会为他们留下新鲜的水果和米饭，点上香，敲响铃铛。这是一种保持联系的方式，让他们知道他们仍然是我们家庭的重要组成部分。

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:41.640
<v Miki Meek>So after the tsunami and earthquake happened, word got out about Itaru's special wind telephone-- that he was using it as another way to stay connected to the dead. Soon, people started showing up randomly on his property, and walking right into the phone booth. This has been going on for five years now. Itaru estimates that thousands of people from all over Japan have come to use his phone.
<v Miki Meek>所以在海啸和地震发生后，佐佐木格的特殊“风之电话”的消息传开了——他正在利用它作为与死者保持联系的另一种方式。很快，人们开始随机出现在他的房产上，径直走进电话亭。这种情况已经持续五年了。佐佐木格估计，来自日本各地的数千人已经来使用他的电话了。

00:04:41.640 --> 00:05:04.210
<v Miki Meek>A TV station asked Itaru, and the people who come to use his phone, if they could videotape their calls from a distance, and put an audio recorder in the phone booth. They wanted to get a sense of how people are still grieving. I watched their documentary after the fifth anniversary of the tsunami back in March. That whole week, all of the news programs in Japan were airing memorial programs.
<v Miki Meek>一家电视台询问佐佐木格以及来使用他电话的人，是否可以从远处拍摄他们的通话，并在电话亭里放置录音机。他们想了解人们是如何继续悲伤的。我在三月份海啸五周年纪念日之后观看了他们的纪录片。那一整周，日本所有的电视新闻节目都在播放纪念节目。

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:18.550
<v Miki Meek>But I found the calls in this particular program remarkable and moving, for just how simple they were. One woman from Otsuchi, named Sachiko Okawa, showed up one afternoon. She's 71-years-old, and lost her husband in the tsunami.
<v Miki Meek>但我发现这个节目中的通话非常引人注目且令人动容，仅仅是因为它们是多么简单。一位来自大槌町名叫大川幸子的女士，一个下午出现在那里。她今年 71 岁，在海啸中失去了丈夫。

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:30.780
<v Miki Meek>She regularly brings her two young grandsons to the phone booth. And you can tell, by how casually they talk to him on the phone. They squish into the phone booth with their grandma, wearing matching blue-and-black-striped shirts.
<v Miki Meek>她经常带着两个年幼的孙子来到电话亭。你可以从他们随意地在电话里跟他说话的方式看出来。他们穿着蓝色和黑色条纹的亲子装，挤在电话亭里和他们的奶奶在一起。

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.100
<v Miki Meek>Sachiko starts the call by picking up the receiver and saying hello.
<v Miki Meek>幸子通过拿起话筒说“喂”开始通话。

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.000
<v Sachiko Okawa>Moshi moshi.
<v Sachiko Okawa>喂，喂。

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:35.900
<v Oldest Grandson>Moshi, moshi.
<v Oldest Grandson>喂，喂。

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.960
<v Miki Meek>Her oldest grandson quickly jumps in.
<v Miki Meek>她的大孙子很快插话。

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:38.930
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:39.610
<v Miki Meek>Hi grandpa.
<v Miki Meek>爷爷好。

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:40.580
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:41.390
<v Miki Meek>How are you?
<v Miki Meek>你好吗？

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:42.580
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.380
<v Miki Meek>I'll be in fourth grade next semester. Wasn't that fast?
<v Miki Meek>我下学期就要上四年级了。是不是很快？

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.490
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:56.530
<v Miki Meek>Daina, my younger brother, he'll be in second grade next year. Then Sachiko corrects him. She says, no, Daina will be in second grade this year, not next.
<v Miki Meek>大奈，我的弟弟，明年要上二年级了。然后幸子纠正了他。她说，不，大奈今年上二年级，不是明年。

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:57.910
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.050
<v Miki Meek>Yeah, this year.
<v Miki Meek>对，今年。

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:12.340
<v Miki Meek>A lot of calls were just like this, straightforward updates about life. The kind of a quick highlights reel you might give to any family member you were catching up with on the phone. The boy, he then tells his grandpa--
<v Miki Meek>很多通话都像这样，是关于生活的直接更新。就像你与任何你在电话中叙旧的家庭成员可能会聊到的快速摘要。那个男孩，他接着告诉他的爷爷——

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.770
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:15.870
<v Miki Meek>Grandma's fine too.
<v Miki Meek>奶奶也很好。

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.020
<v Oldest Grandson>[JAPANESE]
<v Oldest Grandson>[日语]

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.740
<v Miki Meek>I'm giving the phone to Daina now.
<v Miki Meek>我现在把电话给大奈。

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:20.200
<v Daina>[JAPANESE]
<v Daina>[日语]

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.390
<v Miki Meek>Daina, his little brother, grabs the phone.
<v Miki Meek>大奈，他的弟弟，接过了电话。

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:26.340
<v Daina>[JAPANESE]
<v Daina>[日语]

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:30.790
<v Miki Meek>Grandpa, I finished all my homework. Sachiko urges him to keep talking.
<v Miki Meek>爷爷，我的作业全写完了。幸子催促他继续说。

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:31.270
<v Sachiko Okawa>[JAPANESE]
<v Sachiko Okawa>[日语]

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.180
<v Daina>[JAPANESE]
<v Daina>[日语]

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:39.770
<v Miki Meek>He says, everyone is doing fine. Then he hangs up. They all say goodbye.
<v Miki Meek>他说，大家都很好。然后他挂了电话。他们都说了再见。

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.730
<v Sachiko Okawa>Bye, Bye.
<v Sachiko Okawa>拜拜。

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:42.710
<v Sachiko Okawa>[HANGS UP PHONE]
<v Sachiko Okawa>[挂断电话]

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.650
<v Miki Meek>As they're walking out of the booth, Daina says--
<v Miki Meek>当他们走出亭子时，大奈说——

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.120
<v Daina>[JAPANESE]
<v Daina>[日语]

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.730
<v Miki Meek>Maybe Grandpa will say  he heard us.
<v Miki Meek>也许爷爷会说他听到了。

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:56.750
<v Miki Meek>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Miki Meek>[音乐播放]

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:13.200
<v Miki Meek>In another call, a woman in a puffy winter jacket with a fur-lined hood shows up at the booth by herself. Her name is Kikue Hirano, and she's 66. She used to live in Otsuchi. But she moved away after she lost her house and her husband in the tsunami.
<v Miki Meek>在另一个通话中，一位穿着带毛领兜帽的蓬松冬装外套的女士独自出现在亭子里。她的名字叫平野菊惠，今年 66 岁。她过去住在大槌町。但在海啸中失去了房子和丈夫后，她搬走了。

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:23.830
<v Miki Meek>Her husband was a deep-sea fisherman. His name was Miyoji, and they used to talk and drink sake together at night. Now Kikue lives alone, about 50 miles away.
<v Miki Meek>她的丈夫是一名深海渔民。他的名字叫美代治，他们过去常常在晚上一起交谈和喝清酒。现在菊惠独自居住在约 50 英里外的地方。

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:34.360
<v Miki Meek>But sometimes Kikue finds herself driving back to Otsuchi, into this booth. I watched her do this thing that a lot of callers seem to do. You hear Kikue actually dialing the rotary phone--
<v Miki Meek>但有时菊惠发现自己会开车回到大槌町，走进这个亭子。我看着她做很多打电话的人似乎都会做的事情。你听到菊惠在拨打转盘电话——

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.070
<v Kikue Hirano>OK.
<v Kikue Hirano>好的。

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.190
<v Kikue Hirano>--saying some numbers to herself.
<v Kikue Hirano>——自言自语说出一些数字。

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:38.600
<v Kikue Hirano>[KIKUE HUMS]
<v Kikue Hirano>[菊惠哼唱]

00:07:38.600 --> 00:08:02.440
<v Miki Meek>4, 2, 5, 7, 4, 4. She's dialing the phone number for her old house in Otsuchi, the last place she knew to reach her husband. Then Kikue just stands in the booth in silence, holding the phone to her ear.
<v Miki Meek>4，2，5，7，4，4。她在拨打她在大槌町老家的电话号码，那是她知道能联系到丈夫的最后一个地方。然后菊惠就静静地站在亭子里，把电话贴在耳边。

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:21.520
<v Miki Meek>Sometimes she fidgets around and tilts her head up, and concentrates on the ceiling, the same way I do when I want to cry but I'm trying hard not to. It doesn't work. Kikue brushes some tears off her face. Eventually, she hangs up.
<v Miki Meek>有时她会烦躁地动来动去，头向上仰，集中注意力看着天花板，就像我想要哭但又拼命忍住时一样。没用。菊惠擦去脸上的泪水。最终，她挂了电话。

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:57.750
<v Miki Meek>She lingers in the booth a little longer, hands clasped together in front of her, staring at the phone booth floor. She walks out. One pattern that the owner of the phone booth, Itaru, has noticed over the years is that more men than women come to use it. Not surprisingly, this is not a demographic that's known for sharing their feelings, especially the older farmers. They already have a reputation for not talking much.
<v Miki Meek>她在亭子里多逗留了一会儿，双手交握在身前，盯着电话亭的地板。她走了出来。电话亭的主人佐佐木格多年来注意到的一种模式是，男性比女性更常来使用它。不出所料，这并不是一个以分享情感而闻名的群体，尤其是那些老农民。他们已经有了不怎么说话的名声。

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:11.170
<v Miki Meek>In one of the phone calls recorded in winter, a man with gray hair and a little towel hanging around his neck walks into the garden. This kind of towel is part of the uniform of Japanese farmers. They use them to wipe away sweat and clean their hands.
<v Miki Meek>在冬天录下的一通电话中，一位灰头发、脖子上挂着一条小毛巾的男子走进了花园。这种毛巾是日本农民制服的一部分。他们用它擦汗和擦手。

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:23.250
<v Miki Meek>This man opens the telephone booth door. And under his breath, you hear him say, huh, so this is the wind telephone? This appears to be his very first visit to the phone booth.
<v Miki Meek>这个男人打开了电话亭的门。压低声音，你听到他说，呵，这就是风之电话？这似乎是他第一次来电话亭。

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:31.510
<v Miki Meek>He lost his oldest son in the tsunami. His son's name was Nobuyuki. He also lost his house, and had to move into temporary housing with his wife.
<v Miki Meek>他在海啸中失去了长子。他儿子的名字叫信之。他还失去了房子，不得不和妻子住进临时住所。

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:46.840
<v Miki Meek>And recently, she got sick and also passed away. He calls her okaasan. It means "mom" in Japanese. It's what everyone in the family calls the female head of the house, even the husband. It's a very intimate, loving term.
<v Miki Meek>最近，她也生病去世了。他称呼她为“妈妈”（okaasan）。这是日语中“妈妈”的意思。这是家里每个人对女性家长的称呼，连丈夫也一样。这是一个非常亲密、充满爱意的称呼。

